Every paper section cited below was read for this draft from the PDFs in `papers/`. When a claim extrapolates from a paper, the text says so.

## Terms

| Term            | Meaning in this document                                                                        |
| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Segment         | A fixed-size region of the log.                                                                 |
| Head            | The open segment that a writer appends to.                                                      |
| Sealed segment  | A segment that receives no more records.                                                        |
| Live record     | A payload that the current mapping references, or a snapshot that the current index references. |
| Utilization `u` | The live fraction of one segment. LFS uses this term.                                           |
| Fill factor `F` | Live bytes divided by physical capacity. Lomet and Luo use this term.                           |
| Garbage ratio   | Dead bytes divided by used bytes. EBS2 calls it the garbage percentage.                         |
| Free ratio      | Free bytes divided by physical capacity. `F + garbage + free = 1` over capacity.                |
| Victim          | A sealed segment selected for cleaning.                                                         |
| Relocation      | Copying a victim's live records to a head.                                                      |
| Chunk           | A fixed number of payloads compressed together as one zstd frame.                              |
| Publication     | The act of writing a new checkpoint, as ADR-07 defines it.                                      |
| Root            | One of the two checkpoint descriptors. Recovery selects the newest valid root.                  |
| LSN             | Logical sequence number. Every record has one. They never repeat.                               |

## Context

Format 3 appends every write and never reuses space. An overwrite leaves two kinds of dead bytes behind. The first is the old payload block. The second is the old mapping snapshot of every page that the write dirtied, because each publication appends a fresh snapshot per dirty page. Repeated overwrites of a small working set fill the log, and the device returns `ENOSPC` (ADR-03 exhaustion scenario, README "Current limits"). A database on this device has a lifetime measured in bytes written, not in data stored.

ADR-04 proposed replacement-image compaction: quiesce the volume, copy live blocks to a new image, switch. That is an offline operation. ADR-04 says segment reuse is a separate decision. The [prior art review](papers/README.md#2-segment-the-log-and-add-a-serialized-cleaner) sketched a segmented log with a serialized greedy cleaner. It did not choose a concurrency model and did not settle where metadata lives.

One requirement shapes this ADR: reclamation must not stop the world. User writes continue into one segment while other segments are cleaned and later freed. This is the first time the engine needs any concurrency.

Two ideas arrived during review and are now part of the design. The first is to compress data when we relocate it. Alibaba's EBS2 did exactly this in production. The second is discard: without it, live bytes never fall when a filesystem deletes a file, and the cleaner can reclaim only overwrites.

The device targets stateful systems: databases, key-value stores and indexing engines. General filesystem use is a blind-spot check, not the design target.

## What must reclamation preserve?

These invariants come from ADR-04's compaction section and the prior art review. The design below must satisfy them.

1. Never overwrite a physical block while either valid root references it. The replay path from either root counts as a reference.
2. A relocated copy replaces a mapping entry only if that entry still points at the source block at the moment of replacement. Otherwise the cleaner could replace a newer write with an older value.
3. Recovery never applies a record left from a segment's earlier use.
4. User writes never consume the space that the cleaner needs to make progress.
5. One thread mutates the mapping cache, the index, the segment table and the log. ADR-07's dirty-page protection, CLOCK policy and publication all assume this.

## What do the papers establish?

Five results decide the shape of this ADR more than any mechanism does.

### Cleaning cost depends on the victim's utilization

LFS derives the write cost of cleaning as `2 / (1 - u)` for a victim with utilization `u` (§3.4, eq. 1). The factor 2 counts the read of the whole segment. Lomet and Luo write the same cost as `2 / E`, with `E = 1 - u`, and give the extra bytes written per user byte as `(1 - E) / E` (§2.1, eq. 2).

The victim is emptier than the device. LFS §3.5 shows a greedy cleaner at 75% disk utilization cleaning segments that average 55% live (Fig. 4). Lomet and Luo compute, for uniform random overwrites with age-based cleaning, how empty the cleaned segment is at each fill factor (Table 1). Their §4.5 shows that age-based and greedy selection order segments identically in the uniform case.

| Fill factor `F` | Empty fraction `E` of the cleaned segment | Total bytes written per user byte, `1 / E` |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 0.50 | 0.80 | 1.25 |
| 0.70 | 0.53 | 1.89 |
| 0.80 | 0.375 | 2.67 |
| 0.90 | 0.19 | 5.26 |
| 0.95 | 0.094 | 10.6 |

These are analytic values for a harsh model: every page is equally likely to be overwritten. The paper's simulation reproduces them to two digits (§8.1). They set our over-provisioning budget. A device kept at `F ≤ 0.8` writes under 3 bytes per user byte. At `F = 0.95` it writes more than 10.

### Greedy selection is wrong for skewed workloads

LFS §3.5 reports a result that surprised its authors. With 90% of writes going to 10% of files, greedy selection performs *worse* than with uniform writes. Cold segments sit just above the cleaning threshold for a long time and are never cleaned. Cost-benefit selection fixes this. It ranks segments by `(1 - u) × age / (1 + u)`, where age is the modification time of the youngest block. It cleans cold segments at about 75% utilization and hot ones at about 15%, produces the bimodal distribution the cleaner wants (Fig. 6), and cuts write cost by up to 50% versus greedy above 60% disk utilization (Fig. 7).

Lomet and Luo confirm this on 80/20 and 90/10 Zipf distributions and on TPC-C traces, whose skew they measure at roughly 80/20 (§6.2.2, §6.3, Figs. 5 and 6). Under uniform updates all policies coincide (§6.2.1). F2FS uses greedy only for foreground cleaning, to bound latency, and cost-benefit in the background (§2.5). Our seal LSN gives age for free. Cost-benefit belongs in the first delivery, not in a later refinement.

### Background work on the I/O thread drives tail latency

Seltzer et al. measured LFS in a TPC-B workload at 48% disk utilization. LFS was 50% faster than FFS without the cleaner and comparable to FFS with it, a 34% degradation. They attribute the steady-state overhead to segment read time and note that the cleaner cannot run at its optimal rate because transaction response time would be unacceptable (§4, Fig. 7).

Alibaba's EBS paper says the same thing about a modern system. In EBS2 and EBS3 the I/O flow and background tasks ran on the same thread, and "the principal driver of the tail latency is the contention between the IO and the background tasks" (§3.1). Moving I/O to independent threads cut the 99.999th percentile write latency to 1 ms and the read latency to 2.5 ms. Their Base+Burst scheme lets the I/O path preempt bandwidth, CPU and memory from the GC worker under load (§3.2).

Whoever reads the victim pays. If it is the request thread, requests pay.

### Cleaners batch victims and sort what they copy

Nobody cleans one segment at a time in the background. LFS reads "a number of segments into memory, identifies the live data, and writes the live data back to a smaller number of clean segments" (§3.4), tens of segments per pass. With cost-benefit selection it also sorts the live blocks by age before writing them, to keep blocks of different ages apart (§3.5). Lomet and Luo clean 64 segments per cycle, which "amortizes the cost of running the cleaning algorithm over a batch of segments, and also enables more effective separation of pages by update frequency" (§6.1.1). They sort collected pages by estimated update frequency before packing them (§5.3), and their Fig. 4 shows a 16-segment sort buffer reaching near-optimal amplification. EBS2 collects valid data "from the dirtiest DataFiles under the same segment and combining them as new DataFiles" (§2.2). Bitcask merges over all closed files at once. F2FS is the exception, one section per pass, because its foreground cleaner runs on the request path (§2.5).

### Compression at relocation works, and compression on the write path is expensive

EBS2 appends user writes uncompressed with three-way replication. Its garbage collector reads valid data from the dirtiest files and rewrites it as erasure-coded, LZ4-compressed files with an offset table from LBA to compressed location (§2.2, "GC with EC/Compression"). The measured LZ4 ratio was 43.3% to 54.7%, 50.1% on average. Space per byte fell from 3 replicas to 0.69 after GC and 1.29 on average, because data awaiting GC is still replicated. The garbage collector prefers replicated files over already-compressed ones as victims, "to make up more storage space for incoming writes".

The same paper explains why they did not compress on the write path at first. Compressing a 16 KiB block with latency-optimized LZ4 costs 25 µs of CPU, 25.6% of their 100 µs write latency target, and 4,000 MiB/s of throughput needs at least 8 cores (§2.2 "Limitations", §5.2). EBS3 moved compression online only with an FPGA and a Fusion Write Engine that merges writes from many disks until 16 KiB accumulate (§2.3). Even then, in clusters where 4 KiB writes are rare they append them uncompressed, because merging raised the 99th percentile write latency by 220%. Their later ARM-based servers use hardware compression units, and their cold-data experiments with zstd gained a further 17% over LZ4 (§5.2).

Logical Disk made the same suggestion in 1993: "it may be a better strategy to only compress cold files during cleaning; under such a strategy writes and reads of recently written data can be performed at the maximum disk bandwidth" (§2.2).

## Which approach?

Three approaches were compared. They differ on one axis: who does the cleaner's I/O and who mutates state.

| | 1. Serialized steps | 2. Cleaner thread, single writer | 3. Concurrent cleaner |
|---|---|---|---|
| Who reads victims | foreground thread, in bounded steps | cleaner thread | cleaner thread |
| Who compresses | foreground thread | cleaner thread | cleaner thread |
| Who writes copies and updates mappings | foreground thread | foreground thread | cleaner thread, under a lock |
| Open segments | 1 | 2 (user head, relocation head) | 2 or more |
| Shared state | none | one immutable read view plus one bounded channel | mapping cache, index, segment table, LSN counter, both rings |
| Foreground cost | victim reads, compression and copy writes on the request path | copy writes only | lock contention |
| Cleaning while idle | needs a timer | needs a wake-up for the apply step | free |
| A cleaner bug can corrupt data | yes | no: the writer re-validates every proposal | yes |
| Closest prior art | Sprite LFS cleaner (§3.4) | F2FS background cleaning (§2.5); EBS2 frontend/backend split (§2.2) | F2FS multi-head logging (§2.4), extrapolated to a second writer |

**Decision: approach 2.** A cleaner thread reads victims, decides liveness, compresses and batches. The single writer thread validates and applies. The cleaner behaves like a client of the engine that submits large conditional write batches.

**Approach 1 is rejected** because every victim read and every compression lands on the request path. Seltzer measured the cost in 1995 and Alibaba measured it again in 2024. Modern hosts have many CPUs; the hot path should stay on one and the background work should use another. Approach 1's apply path is still built first, because it is approach 2's apply path with the reads done inline; see the deliveries.

**Approach 3 is rejected** because it breaks invariant 5. Two writers mean locks around the mapping cache, the index, the segment table and the LSN counter, and a recovery that merges two independently written streams. Nothing measured says the single writer is the bottleneck.

## Where do mapping snapshots and the segment table live?

Three placements are possible for the metadata that the cleaner has to keep consistent.

- **Mixed segments.** Snapshot records and segment-table records go through the same heads as data, as they do today. The cleaner relocates live snapshots exactly like payloads. Metadata is a small share of bytes: one 4 KiB snapshot per 336 payload blocks when pages fill. Under scattered writes with a small cache it approaches one snapshot per payload (ADR-07 consequences). Either way it dies quickly and mostly sits in good victims. LFS writes its inode map and segment usage table into the log and points to them from the checkpoint region (§3.6, §4.1). This is that design.
- **Dedicated metadata segments.** F2FS separates node and data logs because they have different update frequencies (§2.4, Table 1). Here it would mean a third head, per-kind segment accounting, and a cleaner that handles two segment classes. It removes no mechanism; it duplicates one. INFLOW measured that going from 2 to 6 append streams in F2FS raised filesystem write amplification by up to 33% and device-level GC amplification from 1.82 to 2.19 under Zipf 0.8 (§4.1, Table 1). More streams are not free on a stacked log.
- **In-place slots.** ADR-04's mutable arenas. F2FS does this for its NAT and SIT: two copies, selected per block by bitmaps in the checkpoint pack, plus a small journal of recent entries inside the pack to avoid rewriting 4 KiB blocks for a handful of changes (§2.7). This is incompatible with ADR-07's copy-on-write snapshot pages, which are why our checkpoints are cheap.

Decision: mixed segments.

### How is the segment table stored?

This is the proposal. The segment table is an array with one small entry per segment. The entry holds the segment's state: `Free`, `Open`, `Sealed` or `PendingFree`. Nothing else is persistent; live bytes are RAM-only and rebuilt at open.

The array is stored as `SegmentTablePage` records in the log, about a thousand entries per 4 KiB page. The exact entry width is a delivery detail. These pages work exactly like ADR-07's mapping pages:

1. When a segment changes state, the writer updates the entry in RAM and marks that table page dirty.
2. At publication, the writer appends each dirty table page as a new record and points the candidate descriptor at the new block.
3. Unchanged pages keep their old pointers. Old page records stay where they are until the cleaner relocates or frees them.
4. Recovery reads the pages that the selected descriptor points to.

The pointers live inline in the checkpoint descriptor, which has 4,028 spare bytes in format 3. One `u32` pointer per page gives room for about a thousand pages, which is about a million segments. At 8 MiB per segment that covers 8 TiB of backing, the format maximum. No indirection is needed.

The cost is one 4 KiB table-page write per dirty page per publication. A 128 MiB volume has one table page, so every publication that changed any segment state writes one extra block. A 4 TiB volume with 16 MiB segments has 256 pages; a publication touches the few pages that hold the segments that changed.

### Refinement: a state-change journal in the descriptor

This is not part of the proposal. It is a later optimization, to be added only if the checkpoint I/O counters show table-page writes mattering. It is written down here because it fits the same spare bytes and should not be designed out.

The observation: between two publications, few segments change state. Each head seals one segment and opens one when it fills. The cleaner marks a few segments `PendingFree`, and a few move from `PendingFree` to `Free`. That is perhaps five to twenty changes, each one small integer. Writing a whole 4 KiB page to record twenty 4-byte changes is wasteful.

F2FS has the same problem with its segment information table (SIT) and node address table (NAT), and solves it this way (§2.7). Its checkpoint pack has spare room. Recent SIT and NAT changes are written as a short list inside the checkpoint pack itself; F2FS calls the list a journal. The SIT and NAT blocks themselves are rewritten only when the journal is full. Recovery loads the blocks and then applies the journal entries on top of them.

For us it would work like this:

1. After the pointer array, the descriptor's remaining spare bytes hold a list of `(segment id, new state)` pairs. At 8 bytes per pair, a few hundred fit.
2. At publication, the writer checks how many state changes have happened since the table pages were last written. If they fit in the journal, it writes them into the candidate descriptor and appends no table page. The descriptor is written anyway, so the journal costs no extra I/O.
3. When the journal would overflow, the writer appends the dirty table pages, which already contain every journaled change, and empties the journal.
4. Recovery loads the table pages, then applies the journal entries in order.

One subtlety is why this stays a refinement. The two roots alternate, and each root must be recoverable on its own. So the journal in a descriptor must contain every change since the table pages *that descriptor points to* were written, not just the changes since the previous publication. The simplest rule is to let the journal accumulate until the next full page write, and to copy the whole accumulated journal into each descriptor. Both descriptors point at the same pages until pages are rewritten, so both journals are complete for their pages. That is a second encoding of the same state plus an ordering rule at recovery. The gain is one block per publication on a small volume and a few blocks on a large one. Measure first.

### The mapping index moves into the log too

Format 3 keeps two reserved index bodies after the descriptors, sized by logical capacity, and rewrites the complete body at every publication. ADR-07 accepted that cost and named the fix as future work: copy-on-write index pages in the log, published through the descriptor. The segment table pages above are exactly that mechanism, so format 4 applies it to the index as well.

The index takes the shape of an ext2 inode's block map: a fixed array of direct pointers, then a small array of indirect pointers. Both arrays are present in every descriptor and are mostly zero.

The pieces are these. A mapping page covers 336 blocks, which is 1.3 MiB of logical space. A leaf `IndexPage` lists 1,008 mapping pages, so one leaf covers 1.3 GiB. An indirect `IndexPage` lists 1,008 leaves, so one indirect page covers 1.3 TiB.

The descriptor holds 64 direct pointers and 8 indirect pointers. The direct pointers reach the first 64 leaves, which cover 82.7 GiB. A volume up to that size never needs an indirect page. The 8 indirect pointers reach 8,064 more leaves, which cover 10.2 TiB. The index can therefore address 10.3 TiB of logical space, above the 8 TiB format maximum. The numbers 64 and 8 are choices. More direct pointers cover more space without indirect pages, but each one takes a slot from the segment table pointers described below.

This shape is chosen over a depth field because nothing ever changes shape. A 128 MiB volume has 98 mapping pages, one leaf and one direct pointer, and pays one block per publication, as format 3 did. A volume that grows past the direct range fills the first indirect slot at its next publication; there is no transition from "root points at leaves" to "root points at indirect pages" for recovery to handle. The validator is two loops over one layout. ext4 replaced this map with extents, which is the wrong direction for a block map with a checksum per block; ext2 and ext3 are the reference.

Publication changes in one way: instead of writing the whole body, it appends the dirty leaves, then the dirty indirect pages that point at them, then the descriptor. A leaf is dirty when any of its 1,008 mapping-page pointers changed since the last publication. The three fsync barriers stay where ADR-07 put them. Recovery loads the direct leaves and the indirect pages from the selected descriptor and validates them the way it validates mapping pages today, then streams the mapping pages as before. The resident index in RAM is unchanged.

Three consequences follow:

- The log starts at block 2. Nothing between the descriptors and the first segment depends on logical size. The minimum backing is two blocks plus the segments.
- Publication cost at scale drops from the whole index to the dirty part of it. At 4 TiB, format 3 writes 12.8 MB of index per publication; format 4 writes 4 KiB per leaf touched, at most about 3,170 leaves and in practice the leaves that the publication's dirty mapping pages fall into.
- The cleaner relocates live index pages like snapshots: a leaf or indirect page is live while the current tree references it. Relocating a leaf dirties its parent, which the next publication rewrites.

ADR-04's logical growth then needs only a `GrowLogical` record and a larger tree at the next publication. Its fixed root pointers, `FORMAT` record and checkpoint arenas are not needed. That revision of ADR-04 is still separate; this ADR only makes it small.

## Design

Byte offsets and exact encodings belong to the delivery plan.

### What is a segment?

The log starts at block 2, right after the two descriptors, and is divided into whole segments of `segment_blocks` 4 KiB blocks, fixed at format time. Segment `s` starts at block `2 + s × segment_blocks`. The two-block offset means segments are not aligned to the device's erase blocks; if a discard measurement shows that this matters, the format can reserve the first segment for the descriptors instead. Records do not cross a segment boundary. When a record does not fit in the remaining space of a head's segment, the writer appends a **link record** and moves the head to a free segment.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Free: format or Provision record
    Free --> Open: writer opens the segment as a head
    Open --> Sealed: record does not fit, link record written
    Sealed --> PendingFree: cleaner relocated every live record, publication G
    Sealed --> PendingFree: live bytes reached zero without cleaning, publication G
    PendingFree --> Free: publication G+1
    Free --> Free: discard to the backing
```

`PendingFree` is F2FS's pre-free section (§2.5, step 3): "a victim section is registered as a candidate to become a new free section... After a checkpoint is made, the section finally becomes a free section, to be reallocated. We do this because if a pre-free section is reused before checkpointing, the file system may lose the data referenced by a previous checkpoint when unexpected power outage occurs." The zero-live shortcut is LFS §3.6: "If the count falls to zero then the segment can be reused without cleaning." The writer allocates only `Free` segments.

Segment size is a backing-derived constant, not a logical one. The literature spans 512 KiB to 1 MiB for Sprite on 1990 disks (§3.2), 2 MiB in Lomet and Luo's simulator (§6.1.1), 4 to 32 MiB objects in LSVD (§3.1), and 512 MiB data files inside 32 GiB segments in EBS2 (§2.2). INFLOW §4.2 adds that on a stacked log the upper layer's segment size should be chosen relative to the lower layer's. The [capacity floor](#how-much-backing-does-a-128-mib-volume-need) below argues for 8 or 16 MiB at small sizes. The largest record is 339 blocks, so even 8 MiB (2,048 blocks) holds six of them. A write-buffer group is already not atomic and may span segments.

### Which records change?

The types below are decoded Rust types, as in ADR-07. The offset in each comment is the on-disk layout: little-endian, every unused byte zero and covered by the checksum. The codec writes and reads every field explicitly. No type is `#[repr(C)]`, none is cast onto a disk buffer, and the compiler's field ordering is irrelevant because the compiler never sees the disk bytes. Enums are `#[repr(u8)]` for their stored values only; decoding matches on the number and rejects unknown values.

```rust
/// The first 32 bytes of every record, unchanged from format 3.
pub struct RecordHeader {
    // 0..4   magic "VBLF"
    // 4      format version: 4
    pub kind: RecordKind,       // 5
    // 6..8   zero
    pub volume_id: u64,         // 8
    pub lsn: u64,               // 16: strictly increasing across both heads
    pub previous_boundary: u32, // 24: footer block of the previous record, in any segment
    pub boundary: u32,          // 28: footer block of this record
}

#[repr(u8)]
pub enum RecordKind {
    Data = 1,             // unchanged: 1 to 338 raw 4 KiB payloads, then a footer
    MappingPage = 2,      // unchanged: one 336-entry snapshot
    SegmentTablePage = 3, // one block: states of 504 consecutive segments
    SegmentLink = 4,      // one block: a head moves to another segment
    Provision = 5,        // one block: the backing grew (ADR-04)
    CompressedData = 6,   // zstd chunks, then a footer that indexes them
    Discard = 7,          // one block: LBA ranges the client released
    IndexPage = 8,        // one block: a leaf or indirect page of the mapping index
}

// Every one-block metadata record ends the way a mapping page does:
//   4072..4080  header checksum: XXH3-64 over bytes 0..40, so that recovery can
//               traverse a record whose body is damaged
//   4080..4088  zero
//   4088..4096  record checksum: XXH3-64 over bytes 0..4088 with this field zero
```

```rust
/// The persistent state of 504 consecutive segments. Page `p` covers segments
/// `504 * p .. 504 * p + 504`. Entries past the last segment are zero.
pub struct SegmentTablePage {
    pub header: RecordHeader,         // 0..32, kind = SegmentTablePage
    pub page_id: u32,                 // 32
    // 36..40 zero
    pub entries: [SegmentEntry; 504], // 40..4072, 8 bytes each
    // 4072..4096 metadata trailer
}

pub struct SegmentEntry {
    pub state: SegmentState,  // 0
    pub head: u8,             // 1: 1-based head index while Open; 0 otherwise
    // 2..4 zero
    pub seal_generation: u32, // 4: checkpoint generation current when the segment was sealed;
                              //    the cleaner's age input. Zero while Free or Open.
}

#[repr(u8)]
pub enum SegmentState {
    Free = 0,
    Open = 1,
    Sealed = 2,
    PendingFree = 3,
}
```

Live bytes are not in the entry. They are rebuilt at open from the mapping pages that recovery already streams. The seal generation is in the entry because nothing else records it: cost-benefit selection needs each sealed segment's age before the cleaner has read it, and ties within one generation are broken by segment number.

```rust
/// One page of the mapping index. A leaf points at MappingPage records; an
/// indirect page points at leaves. Leaf `p` covers mapping pages
/// `1008 * p .. 1008 * p + 1008`. Leaves 0..64 are reached through the
/// descriptor's direct pointers; leaf `64 + 1008 * q + i` is entry `i` of the
/// indirect page in descriptor slot `q`. Zero means "nothing below". Pointers
/// past the volume's last page are zero.
pub struct IndexPage {
    pub header: RecordHeader,  // 0..32, kind = IndexPage
    pub page_id: u32,          // 32: position within its level
    pub level: IndexLevel,     // 36
    // 37..40 zero
    pub pointers: [u32; 1008], // 40..4072
    // 4072..4096 metadata trailer
}

#[repr(u8)]
pub enum IndexLevel {
    Leaf = 1,
    Indirect = 2,
}
```

The 64 direct leaves cover 64,512 mapping pages, which is 82.7 GiB. The 8 indirect pages cover 8,064 leaves, which is 8.13 million mapping pages, or 10.2 TiB. The index is not the limit of format 4.

```rust
/// One 4 KiB block at physical block 0 or 1. Format 3's reserved index bodies
/// and their checksum are gone; the index is a tree of IndexPage records
/// rooted here. The log starts at block 2.
pub struct CheckpointDescriptor {
    // 0..4   magic "VBLC"
    // 4      format version: 4
    pub slot: CheckpointSlot,          // 5: green 0, blue 1
    // 6..8   zero
    pub volume_id: u64,                // 8
    pub logical_block_size: u32,       // 16: 4096
    pub volume_blocks: u32,            // 20: grows only through a future GrowLogical record
    pub backing_blocks: u64,           // 24: the provisioned bound, grown by Provision records
    pub checkpoint_lsn: u64,           // 32
    pub generation: u64,               // 40
    pub segment_blocks: u32,           // 48: segment size in blocks, fixed at format
    pub head_count: u8,                // 52: 1 or 2 today, at most 4
    // 53..56 zero
    pub head_boundary: [u32; 4],       // 56..72: last record boundary of each head; zero = unused.
                                       //         The head's open segment is (boundary - 2) / segment_blocks.
    pub direct_leaf: [u32; 64],        // 72..328: blocks of leaf IndexPage records 0..64; zero = no leaf
    pub indirect: [u32; 8],            // 328..360: blocks of indirect IndexPage records; zero = unused
    pub table_page_count: u32,         // 360: ceil(segment count / 504)
    // 364..368 zero
    pub table_page_block: [u32; 930],  // 368..4088: block of each SegmentTablePage record
    // 4088..4096 descriptor checksum
}
```

The segment table is the limit of format 4. The 930 table pointers reach 930 pages. Each page holds 504 entries, so the table describes 468,720 segments. With 16 MiB segments that is 7.2 TiB of backing. With 8 MiB segments it is 3.6 TiB. With 32 MiB segments it is 14.3 TiB. A backing larger than the table allows needs a larger segment size, or a tree for the table like the one the index uses. Neither is needed now. The state-change journal described as a refinement above would take its bytes from the table pointer array, which is another reason it is not in the proposal.

```rust
/// Closes a head's segment and names the next one. It is the last record in
/// the segment it closes. The first record of the next segment has
/// `previous_boundary` equal to this record's `boundary`.
pub struct SegmentLink {
    pub header: RecordHeader, // 0..32, kind = SegmentLink
    pub next_segment: u32,    // 32
    pub head: u8,             // 36: 1-based head index
    // 37..4072 zero
    // 4072..4096 metadata trailer
}

/// Records that the backing grew. Replay applies it before any later record
/// may reference a segment in the new range. Idempotent by target bound.
pub struct Provision {
    pub header: RecordHeader,          // 0..32, kind = Provision
    pub previous_backing_blocks: u64,  // 32
    pub new_backing_blocks: u64,       // 40
    // 48..4072 zero
    // 4072..4096 metadata trailer
}

/// LBA ranges the client released. Replay unmaps them in LSN order. A discard
/// is durable after the next successful FLUSH, like a write.
pub struct Discard {
    pub header: RecordHeader,        // 0..32, kind = Discard
    pub range_count: u32,            // 32: 1 to 504
    // 36..40 zero
    pub ranges: [DiscardRange; 504], // 40..4072, 8 bytes each; unused entries zero
    // 4072..4096 metadata trailer
}

pub struct DiscardRange {
    pub start_lba: u32,   // 0
    pub block_count: u32, // 4
}
```

```rust
/// A CompressedData record is `chunk_count` zstd frames concatenated and padded
/// to whole blocks, followed by this footer. Chunk `c` holds payloads
/// `c * chunk_payloads .. (c + 1) * chunk_payloads` of the LBA array. LBAs are sorted.
pub struct CompressedDataFooter {
    pub header: RecordHeader,       // 0..32, kind = CompressedData
    pub payload_count: u16,         // 32: 1 to 304
    pub chunk_count: u16,           // 34: ceil(payload_count / chunk_payloads)
    pub chunk_payloads: u8,         // 36: payloads per chunk, 4 to 32; 16 initially
    pub algorithm: Compression,     // 37
    // 38..40 zero
    pub lbas: [u32; 304],           // 40..1256
    pub checksums: [u64; 304],      // 1256..3688: XXH3-64 of the uncompressed payload, seeded by
                                    //   volume_id, over LE(lba) || payload. Location-free, so the
                                    //   cleaner computes it once and the writer copies it.
    pub chunks: [ChunkEntry; 19],   // 3688..3992, 16 bytes each
    // 3992..4072 zero
    // 4072..4096 metadata trailer
}

pub struct ChunkEntry {
    pub offset: u32,   // 0: byte offset of the zstd frame from the record's first block
    pub length: u32,   // 4: compressed length in bytes
    pub checksum: u64, // 8: XXH3-64 over the frame, seeded by volume_id, over
                       //    LE(footer block, chunk index) || frame. Location-bound; the
                       //    writer computes it at apply, when it knows the footer block.
}

#[repr(u8)]
pub enum Compression {
    Zstd = 1,
}
```

Two checksums per chunk replace format 3's one location-bound checksum per payload. The payload checksum is bound to the LBA only, so the cleaner can compute it before anyone knows where the record will land. The chunk checksum is bound to the footer block and the chunk index, so a misdirected or torn write of the compressed bytes is detected before decompression, and the writer computes it at apply over the compressed bytes without decompressing anything. With 16-payload chunks the footer holds 304 payloads and 19 chunks. The record's data blocks hold `ceil(sum of length / 4096)` blocks, about half of 304 at 2:1. The two-block-footer alternative in the open questions would raise the payload count to about 630.

```rust
/// The mapping entry does not change size. Bit 31 of the physical address is new.
pub const COMPRESSED_FLAG: u32 = 1 << 31;

/// physical == 0:                     unmapped; checksum must be zero
/// physical & COMPRESSED_FLAG == 0:   block `physical` holds the raw payload
/// physical & COMPRESSED_FLAG != 0:   block `physical & !COMPRESSED_FLAG` is a
///                                    CompressedDataFooter; find the LBA in it
pub struct MappingEntry {
    pub physical: u32,
    pub checksum: u64,
}
```

These are in RAM only. Containers are shown as `Vec` for clarity; the implementation preallocates them to the bounds the design states.

